+On a self-hosted runner the workflow first checks for Xcode and those commands and says what is missing. It skips its `brew install` steps there, because Homebrew no longer has prebuilt packages for Intel Macs and they would compile LLVM, GCC and Ruby for hours. It puts the tools it downloads in a per-job directory instead of `sudo` into /usr/local, lays out the DMG without Finder (`create-dmg --skip-jenkins`), and always deletes its temporary signing keychain. On GitHub's machines it behaves as before. Without a Linux runner, the small Linux jobs of each build (setup, bridge, cleanup, about 10 minutes) still run on GitHub, so keep the Actions budget above $0 or set `RDGEN_RUNNER_LINUX` too. An Intel Mac cannot build Apple Silicon clients with this workflow; Intel clients do run on Apple Silicon Macs through Rosetta 2, which Apple supports fully through macOS 27. The first build compiles every dependency and is slow; later ones reuse the caches. Only you can start builds in a private repository, but the runner runs them with that account's rights, so don't use it for public repositories.
 
